<!--{"title": "MCP Apps and tool annotations: governing agent UIs and actions", "description": "MCP tools can now return interactive UIs, and annotations hint at tool risk. How MCP Apps and tool annotations work, what they can't guarantee, and how I govern them.", "h1": "MCP Apps and tool annotations: how to govern what agents show and do", "kicker": "AI architecture · MCP", "lede": "Two MCP features change what enterprise agents look like. MCP Apps let tools return interactive interfaces inside the conversation. Tool annotations describe how risky a tool is. Both are useful, and both need governance the protocol can't provide on its own.", "date": "2026-10-21", "datetime": "2026-10-21T04:15:00+04:00", "readtime": "8 min read", "format": "explainer", "tags": ["ai", "mcp", "agents", "security", "governance"], "keywords": ["MCP Apps", "Model Context Protocol UI", "MCP tool annotations", "readOnlyHint destructiveHint", "agent governance", "MCP security", "agentic UI", "enterprise AI agents", "lethal trifecta MCP", "AI architect"], "faq": [["What are MCP Apps?", "The first official MCP extension, announced as live in January 2026. Tools can declare a UI resource that the host renders in a sandboxed iframe, so tools can return dashboards, forms and other interactive components inside the conversation."], ["What are MCP tool annotations?", "Optional hints a server attaches to a tool, such as readOnlyHint, destructiveHint, idempotentHint and openWorldHint. The specification says they are hints and clients must treat them as untrusted unless they come from a trusted server."], ["Can I rely on tool annotations for security?", "Not on their own. They are useful inputs to confirmation prompts and policy engines, especially for servers you control, but enforcement should happen in a gateway or host policy that combines them with other signals."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/", "/technical-due-diligence/"], "citations": [{"title": "MCP Apps: bringing UI capabilities to MCP clients", "url": "https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-01-26-mcp-apps/", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol blog", "date": "26 Jan 2026"}, {"title": "Tool annotations as risk vocabulary: what hints can and can't do", "url": "https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-03-16-tool-annotations/", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol blog", "date": "16 Mar 2026"}, {"title": "Tools (specification 2025-11-25)", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol"}, {"title": "MCP Apps extension documentation", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/extensions/apps", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol"}, {"title": "The lethal trifecta for AI agents", "url": "https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/", "publisher": "Simon Willison", "date": "16 Jun 2025"}]}-->
<p>Enterprise agents are moving beyond chat. A tool that queries a database no longer has to return hundreds of rows as text; it can return a table the user can sort and filter. A deployment tool can present a form instead of asking twenty questions. At the same time, organisations are connecting agents to more tools from more servers, and need a way to tell a harmless lookup from a destructive action.</p>
<p>MCP has answers to both: MCP Apps and tool annotations. Here is how they work and how I fit them into an enterprise architecture.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>MCP Apps: interfaces inside the conversation</h2>
<p>In January 2026 the MCP maintainers announced MCP Apps as the first official MCP extension. A tool declares a UI resource, served under a <code>ui://</code> scheme, and the host fetches it and renders it in a sandboxed iframe. The UI and host communicate over JSON-RPC via <code>postMessage</code>, so the interface can call server tools and update the model's context as the user interacts<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/mcp-apps-tool-annotations/mcp-app-color-picker.webp" alt="A colour picker MCP App rendered inside a chat conversation, with hex, RGB and HSL values and a Select Color button" width="1080" height="836" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>An example MCP App: a tool returns an interactive colour picker that renders inside the conversation. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-01-26-mcp-apps/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP blog, MCP Apps: Bringing UI Capabilities To MCP Clients</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The announcement lists support across several major clients, and builds on earlier work from MCP-UI and OpenAI's Apps SDK<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. For enterprise teams, the appeal is one interactive tool that works in several AI clients without client-specific code.</p>
<p>The security model has several layers: sandboxed iframes, templates the host can review before rendering, loggable messages between UI and host, and optional user consent for tool calls the UI initiates<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The maintainers are clear that users should still vet servers before connecting them.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Tool annotations: a risk vocabulary, not a guarantee</h2>
<p>Tool annotations let a server describe a tool's behaviour with four hints: whether it is read-only, destructive, idempotent, or reaches an open world of external entities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The defaults are deliberately cautious: a tool with no annotations is assumed to be able to modify things, possibly destructively, and to reach outside its environment<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The key word is hint. The specification says annotations aren't guaranteed to describe behaviour faithfully, and clients must treat them as untrusted unless they come from a trusted server<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. A malicious server can label a destructive tool read-only.</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Annotations can</th><th>Annotations can't</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Drive confirmation prompts for destructive tools</td><td>Prove a tool behaves as described</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feed a policy engine as one input among several</td><td>Replace enforcement in a gateway or host</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support graduated trust for internal servers you control</td><td>Make an untrusted third-party server safe</td></tr>
<tr><td>Help users understand tools before running them</td><td>Detect risk that comes from combining tools</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Risk lives in the session, not the tool</h2>
<p>The MCP maintainers' post on annotations makes a point I agree with strongly: risk often comes from combinations. Drawing on Simon Willison's lethal trifecta of private data, untrusted content and external communication<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>, it notes that users routinely combine tools from several servers, so the risk profile belongs to the session rather than any single server<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Several open proposals aim to add metadata so clients can detect when a session has all three legs available<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>This is the same containment logic as in <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence for agents</a>. MCP Apps add a wrinkle: a UI that can call tools and update model context is another path by which actions happen, and it needs the same policies as the model.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I govern them in an enterprise</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Approve servers, not just tools.</strong> Maintain an allow-list of MCP servers. Annotations from internal, reviewed servers can drive policy; from others they are informational only.</li>
<li><strong>Require annotations on internal servers</strong>, reviewed in code review like any other interface contract, and test that tools behave as annotated.</li>
<li><strong>Enforce at a gateway.</strong> Route tool calls, including those initiated from MCP App UIs, through a policy layer that checks user rights, tool risk and session state, as in <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Track session state.</strong> Once a session has read private data and seen untrusted content, block or require approval for open-world and destructive tools.</li>
<li><strong>Review UI templates</strong> for MCP Apps before approving a server, and log every UI-to-host message.</li>
<li><strong>Design approvals people actually read.</strong> Show the exact action and data affected. A confirmation dialog users click through by reflex is not a control.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p>Treat annotations as labels and the gateway as the lock. Labels help people and policies make decisions; only enforcement outside the model and the server stops a mislabelled tool.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where this is heading</h2>
<p>The annotation vocabulary is deliberately small today, with several proposals open for trust, sensitivity and output-safety hints<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Build your policy engine so new hints can be added as inputs without redesign, and record your choices in an <a href="/insights/architecture-decision-records-fractional-cto/">architecture decision record</a>.</p>
</section>

